WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.120
How does one cell push on a sound wave, many thousands of times a second?

2
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:09.270
The chain has five links.

3
00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.110
Sound bends the hair bundle back and forth.

4
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.030
Bending toward the tallest stereocilia opens more transduction channels.

5
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.350
Current flows in, so the voltage rises and falls with the bundle.

6
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.510
The voltage moves charge inside prestin, which changes shape.

7
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.460
The cell shortens and lengthens in step, and pushes on the organ of Corti.

8
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:36.843
Why voltage? A 20 kHz tone completes a cycle every 50 microseconds.

9
00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:43.217
An amplifier working on every cycle cannot wait on a slow chemical step, such as ATP

10
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:44.217
hydrolysis.

11
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:46.197
Voltage has limits too.

12
00:00:47.077 --> 00:00:49.277
The membrane smooths fast voltage changes.

13
00:00:50.197 --> 00:00:55.077
In simple estimates, its cutoff lies far below the highest frequencies basal cells

14
00:00:55.077 --> 00:00:58.997
amplify. How much that limits the amplifier is still argued.

15
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.640
How can you watch prestin's charge move?

16
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.560
Measure capacitance, the charge moved per change in voltage.

17
00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:11.333
In the simplest model, each prestin flips between two states, and voltage tilts the

18
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:16.433
odds. So its charge moves along an S-shaped curve, mostly near its midpoint.

19
00:01:17.017 --> 00:01:19.137
The slope of an S peaks at its middle.

20
00:01:19.537 --> 00:01:23.637
So the extra capacitance is a bell, called nonlinear capacitance.

21
00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:28.557
Its steepness is set by z, the effective charge that moves per prestin.

22
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.340
Predict. If z doubles, does the bell get wider or narrower?

23
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:42.633
Narrower. A larger z makes the S steeper.

24
00:01:43.473 --> 00:01:45.813
The width of the bell scales as one over z.

25
00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:49.663
Take the gerbil fit.

26
00:01:50.503 --> 00:01:55.923
At 50 mV above the midpoint, about 80% % of prestin is in the contracted state.

27
00:01:56.723 --> 00:01:59.083
The bell there is 64% % of its peak.

28
00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:03.683
This reads charge movement, not the force or power a hair cell produces.

29
00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:07.947
Structures of purified prestin suggest an area motor.

30
00:02:08.807 --> 00:02:11.727
But each is a candidate end state, not a working motor.

31
00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:13.497
Is the motor needed?

32
00:02:14.337 --> 00:02:18.137
In a prestin knock-in mouse, transduction and cell mechanics were normal.

33
00:02:18.937 --> 00:02:20.517
Yet amplification was lost.

34
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:23.657
So the cell body's motility is necessary.

35
00:02:24.617 --> 00:02:28.057
In an excised frog organ, a hair bundle can also add energy.

36
00:02:28.717 --> 00:02:33.037
How the work is split between bundle and cell body in mammals has not been

37
00:02:33.037 --> 00:02:34.037
established.

38
00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:35.507
Take this with you.

39
00:02:35.507 --> 00:02:38.387
Bundle, channel, voltage, prestin, push.

40
00:02:38.387 --> 00:02:41.627
The bell is the motor's electrical fingerprint.
